      "first_30_lines": "\"\"\"\ndependencies.py \u2014 FastAPI Dependency Injection\n\nPYTHON CONCEPT: Dependency Injection in FastAPI\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nFastAPI's `Depends()` is a dependency injection system.\nThink of it like middleware in Express, but more powerful:\n\nExpress:\n    app.use(authMiddleware)          \u2190 runs on every route\n    \nFastAPI:\n    @app.get(\"/\", dependencies=[Depends(get_current_user)])   \u2190 per route\n    async def route(user = Depends(get_current_user)):         \u2190 or inline\n\nThe difference: FastAPI dependencies return a VALUE that gets\ninjected into your route function as an argument.\n\nHow it works:\n1. Request comes in to a protected route\n2. FastAPI calls `get_current_user()` automatically\n3. `get_current_user` extracts and validates the JWT\n4. Returns the User object (or raises 401 if invalid)\n5. Your route function receives the User object directly\n\nThis is like middleware that returns a value instead of\njust calling next().\n\nPYTHON CONCEPT: HTTPBearer\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500",
